f the normal:\n\nWe find the equation of the normal as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{m}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}(x-\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}x+1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\,x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/b2/b2485a1b1386468904947c52ff3f52997f72597da84384ae41d246f386a39232.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542198 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542094 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542198 #edb/ref 1069 "q-351197" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542198 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542198 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation $y=f(x)$ of the tangent line to the polar curve $r={e}^{-\\theta }$ at $\\theta =0,$ given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of the tangent line is $y=$\n{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542198 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b816908c-853d-5e9e-8dd6-8428f48a97f8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542198 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the equation of the tangent line to a polar curve at a point $P,$ we follow four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\nIn this case, we're given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P:$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =0} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-1}{1+0} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P:$\n\nWe compute the $xy$-coordinates of $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={e}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {y}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={e}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(1,0).$\n\n**Step 4**: Using point-slope form, find the equation of the tangent:\n\nWe find the equation of the tangent as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =-1(x-1) \\\\ y & =-x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/66/6651277bec90a77e19d0bd6c1d1a464f636cf675cae25ad47a6f60a2a352002b.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542202 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542202 #edb/ref 1069 "q-103815" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542202 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542202 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent line to the polar curve $r={e}^{-\\theta }$ at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta )={e}^{-\\theta }(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ),\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-{e}^{-\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542202 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "be4431be-3fe9-5dd2-82ee-71868596c05f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542202 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the equation of the tangent line to a polar curve at a point $P,$ we follow four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\nIn this case, we're given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta )={e}^{-\\theta }(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ),\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-{e}^{-\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-\\theta }(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{{e}^{-\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\cancel{{e}^{-\\theta }}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{\\cancel{{e}^{-\\theta }}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P:$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P:$\n\nWe compute the $xy$-coordinates of $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4} \\\\ {y}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4}).$\n\n**Step 4**: Using point-slope form, find the equation of the tangent:\n\nWe find the equation of the tangent as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4} & =0\\cdot (x-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4}) \\\\ y-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4} & =0 \\\\ y & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/6b/6b8602110483fcc09d9ac0c04a16ab4e624f00a2ff752851e9c027bef5c9bc83.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542205 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542103 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542205 #edb/ref 1069 "q-141419" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542205 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542205 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the equation of the **normal** line to the polar curve $r=1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ at $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ given that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542205 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c7a90aa7-52b9-549e-8c2d-341971525e44" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542205 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the equation of the normal line to a polar curve at a point $P,$ we follow four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\nIn this case, we're given that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P:$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =2\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{2\\pi }{3})}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{2\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\frac{1}{2}-2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})}{2\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P:$\n\nWe compute the $xy$-coordinates of $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}))\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =(1-2(-\\frac{1}{2}))(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ {y}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}))\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =(1-2(-\\frac{1}{2}))\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(-1,\\sqrt{3}).$\n\n**Step 4**: Using point-slope form, find the equation of the normal:\n\nWe find the equation of the normal as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{m}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-\\sqrt{3} & =3\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (x-(-1)) \\\\ y-\\sqrt{3} & =3\\sqrt{3}x+3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ y & =3\\sqrt{3}x+4\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/ba/ba0b04158944bd15aeea3220d4aa63321dd39c423a4f6646ba59c299fa8ef736.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542206 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542206 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542206 #edb/ref 1069 "q-351547" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542206 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542206 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the normal line to the polar curve $r=4\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+4\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ), \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\theta )=2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-4\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of the normal line is $y=$\n{{field-1}} $x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542206 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c83da1a3-053f-5553-bc32-57bdb774a02e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542206 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the equation of the normal line to a polar curve at a point $P,$ we follow four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\nIn this case, we're given that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+4\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )=2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-4\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+4\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-4\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P:$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})+4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})}{2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})-4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2-\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P:$\n\nWe compute the $xy$-coordinates of $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot {(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ {y}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\n**Step 4**: Using point-slope form, find the equation of the normal:\n\nWe find the equation of the normal as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{m}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-\\frac{\\pi }{2} & =\\frac{\\pi -2}{2}(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ y & =\\frac{\\pi -2}{2}\\,x+\\frac{\\pi (4-\\pi )}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/2b/2bb2678804e79eb60cf9e1b16df60b8dad4ec4ccf8b2a9234a838c394ab9f3ac.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542215 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542215 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542087 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542215 #edb/ref 1069 "q-362883" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542215 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542215 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation $y=f(x)$ of the tangent line to the polar curve $r=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡2\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of the tangent line is $y=$\n{{field-1}}$x\\,-$\n{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542215 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e1dd610f-bcb9-5742-82dd-3d71c2887869" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542215 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the equation of the tangent line to a polar curve at a point $P,$ we follow four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\nIn this case, we're given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡2\\theta .\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P:$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /6} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P:$\n\nWe compute the $xy$-coordinates of $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =7\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\\\ {y}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =7{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =7{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{4},\\frac{7}{4}).$\n\n**Step 4**: Using point-slope form, find the equation of the tangent:\n\nWe find the equation of the tangent as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-\\frac{7}{4} & =\\sqrt{3}(x-\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{4}) \\\\ y-\\frac{7}{4} & =\\sqrt{3}x-\\frac{21}{4} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{3}\\,x-\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/4d/4d58fd3550e70915f3bc2a5e8ecd64cc4ef6c154a84ca536a5ca6ef6ddf49450.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542229 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541989 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542229 #edb/ref 1069 "q-149691" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542229 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542229 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent line to the polar curve $r=2\\theta $ at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542229 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f9519db7-867b-512b-ba13-465c2170cdc3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542229 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the equation of the tangent line to a polar curve at a point $P,$ we follow four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\nIn this case, we're given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P:$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 0)}{(0-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P:$\n\nWe compute the $xy$-coordinates of $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =0 \\\\ {y}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,\\pi ).$\n\n**Step 4**: Using point-slope form, find the equation of the tangent:\n\nWe find the equation of the tangent as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-\\pi  & =-\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\cdot (x-0) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{\\pi }x+\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/13/13bb063a7ae79887a653e5f1c330851e56183049f77853a6a1d35d4a023d615c.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
